The diamond problem occurs when multiple parent classes provide implementations of the same method.
Traditional interfaces only define method signatures and do not provide implementations, so there is no ambiguity. 
The implementing class provides the single concrete implementation, which resolves the conflict.

In C#, we avoid multiple inheritance by using composition and interfaces. 
Instead of inheriting behavior from multiple base classes, we inject required services like logging or caching using dependency injection.
This follows the composition-over-inheritance principle and leads to loosely coupled and testable systems.
